samples: DECLINED corpus console principles/exercises/ch22/EXERCISES.md:114 [file]: NOT a missing file. The block is exercise 22-11's D32 demonstration — an unmarked `widest.lu` joining `sum.lu`'s module, E0302 on `main`. A corpus `.lu` must carry `//! check:` and `//! phase:`, and `[conf.directive.standalone]` (D59) makes a file carrying both a standalone entry that never joins its directory's module, so the collision cannot be staged. Measured on wolf 0.2.12 and lupin 0.1.34: the pair runs clean, exit 0 both — wolf-book#29 §2 (premise corrected at bs44), retires at a corpus admission rule that can hold a non-standalone member. The `console,in(pkg/…)` escape hatch is NOT one today, and the reason is an ordering rather than a design: a corpus fence parses `in(…)` (`collect_corpus_console` carries `fi.in_fixture` through), but `console::check` runs the corpus admission gate against the real exercise directory BEFORE any staging, so the block is declined before the fixture is looked at — and if it got that far, `stage()` opens with `remove_dir_all(dir)` and would wipe the staged exercise tree the corpus lane had just put there. Two small fixes, one hole
samples: DECLINED corpus console principles/exercises/ch23/EXERCISES.md:124 [file]: the corpus rule to admit a `--dir` package, not a fixture. None of the six commands carries a `.lu` argument, and `plain_run_of_a_solution` declines any `wolf`/`lupin` command that names no `.lu` on disk — so a checked-in `app/` changes nothing until the rule widens. The package itself already exists as `samples/pkg/shelf/`, which prints this block's tree verbatim at book/ch24.md — wolf-book#29 §2 (premise corrected at bs44), retires at the `--dir` widening for corpus blocks, after which `samples/pkg/shelf` is the fixture
samples: DECLINED corpus console principles/exercises/ch24/EXERCISES.md:136 [file]: the same `--dir` widening as ch23's, and then one more thing: the transcript runs `wolf update` BEFORE `wolf audit --ci` and still expects the ACQUIRES line, which is the opposite of what the page's own prose says refreshing first does. `samples/pkg/acquired/` is the stale-ledger package already, and book/ch24.md prints its ACQUIRES output with no `update` in front of it — wolf-book#29 §2 (premise corrected at bs44), retires at the `--dir` widening, plus a re-reading of the transcript's own ordering
